    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1d21ebf9-a53d-5301-93ca-694ff77a8334",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}+5,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-2,2)$\n- **II.** $x\\in [0,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty,0]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+5$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $5$ units up to get $f(x)={x}^{2}+5.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/7e0dc010b5ea3ce4637f7438e9b707bf5bcaad2d795078438a00cdeb0f02793c-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,2).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/7e0dc010b5ea3ce4637f7438e9b707bf5bcaad2d795078438a00cdeb0f02793c-1.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in [0,\\infty)$ and  $x\\in (-\\infty,0].$ On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/7e0dc010b5ea3ce4637f7438e9b707bf5bcaad2d795078438a00cdeb0f02793c-2.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
