    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6c5fa4bc-88bc-5a11-a207-26df27de0d60",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-6|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,5)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (3,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty,7]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-6|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $6$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-6|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-6|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-6|+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/9239f569ad019bab4cc090a679fd82f68d61670393c11b0e8946145e97f27bc0-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,5).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (3,\\infty)$  and $(-\\infty,7].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "99f82faa-053b-548f-8d39-9032f4f44aa0",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
