  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124474,
      "id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "419da12d-8c4f-5ed1-9e02-40706e1367d7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+5)^{2}+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,-5]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-7,-5]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-6,-4]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=-5$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(-\\infty,-5]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-5\\le -5$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(-7,-5]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-5\\le -5$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-6,-4]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{11}{2}$ and $- \\frac{9}{2}$, both with output $\\frac{9}{4}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "90946b3f-6470-58fc-bcbf-8ce19c5f2e0f",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "46a4ab25-9210-55c9-b17f-76c380aa4fab",
