  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124474,
      "id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e10c40d6-e275-5d59-9b27-97383fa8d2fd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x+2)^{2}-6,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-2,0]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-3,-1]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-2,\\infty)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=-2$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(-2,0]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-2\\ge -2$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(-3,-1]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{5}{2}$ and $- \\frac{3}{2}$, both with output $- \\frac{23}{4}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-2,\\infty)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-2\\ge -2$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ea7a22cb-5f9b-5d04-9e45-d982009115ee",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "ee1e5846-1c64-5b6d-9c22-95f4d6d52f08",
