  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124474,
      "id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5b57a205-188e-51cc-a62d-3f8e61b4c528",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,1)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-1,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty,3]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|+2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-2|$ by $2$ units up to get $f(x)=|x-2|+2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/61d172a39d5c19fd7d29d09127311ebfe709857abe5a7df820d4bd5a5629ae5b-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,1).$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in (-1,\\infty)$  and $(-\\infty,3].$ On each of these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "7a8bc7ce-1f87-50b4-a9e8-d87a46e1a1cb",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
