  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186124474,
      "id": "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d",
      "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "44445176-b8a9-595b-a51c-a146997030a1",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-1|+4,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (0,2]$\n- **II.** $x\\in [1,3)$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty,1]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=1$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(0,2]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$, both with output $\\frac{9}{2}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $[1,3)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $1\\ge 1$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-\\infty,1]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $1\\le 1$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8311c60e-ba9b-5f85-b782-e5c5308d9e62",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "042d6e8e-8aca-5392-8869-e1619e5d2e71",
