    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6d74bd27-b601-5f3c-ab90-dd8cbe3e335a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|-1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (4,6]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (-\\infty,4]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (3,5]$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=4$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(4,6]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $4\\ge 4$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(-\\infty,4]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $4\\le 4$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(3,5]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{7}{2}$ and $\\frac{9}{2}$, both with output $- \\frac{1}{2}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
